INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S PARLOR - NIGHT (1888)

Oil lamps. Wind shaking the shutters. A round table draped in black velvet with a candle at its heart. Six chairs.

MADAME CORDELIA OSTERHOUT, 50s, medium, in a high-collared gown. COMMODORE ARCHIBALD FENN, 60s, whiskers, pocket watch. LADY MIRIAM FENN, 40s, his wife, gloved hands folded. KEEPER ISAIAH TULL, 50s, oilskin coat, scowling. A young MISS THEODORA CRANE, 20s, notebook in her lap. And BARNABY, 12, the keeper's boy, crouched on the stair.

Madame Osterhout closes her eyes. The candle gutters. The table gives a small knock. The Commodore checks his watch. The knock repeats.

MADAME OSTERHOUT
Is there a spirit in this room?

Knock.

COMMODORE FENN
Is that you, Edmund?

Knock.

LADY MIRIAM
Archibald, stop.

COMMODORE FENN ^
Edmund, if it is you, rap three times.

LADY MIRIAM
He is dead, Archibald. Let him be.

Three knocks, steady. Miriam's gloves tighten. Theodora writes. Tull stares at the floorboards. Barnaby's face withdraws from the stair.

KEEPER TULL
That's the tide, sir. The tide moves the rock.

MADAME OSTERHOUT
(eyes closed)
The tide is not what moves tonight.

The candle flame lies flat. A gust blows through the room. The lamps dim. Miriam stands, lifts her chin, and walks out onto the gallery. The door bangs after her.

The Commodore starts to rise. Tull's hand lands on his arm. Madame opens her eyes. She looks at Theodora, who is not writing now.

MADAME OSTERHOUT (CONT'D)
(to Theodora, flat)
You did not tell me she was your mother.

Theodora lowers her pen.
